<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/experimental-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Experimental design is <strong>planning the comparison before collecting the data</strong> —
    the discipline Ronald Fisher built at Rothamsted agricultural station in the 1920s and 30s,
    where decades of field trials met new statistics. Its law: the quality of a conclusion is
    decided at design time; no analysis can rescue a confounded experiment. But the experiment
    is only the last stage of a chain that starts earlier: define the population, choose the
    sampling scheme, write the questions, classify the variables — every link is a decision
    that can quietly invalidate what follows. This chapter walks the whole chain.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Fisher's three pillars plus the factorial upgrade" widget="equationStep" config={doeCfg} />

  <h2>Population, sample and census</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>population</strong> is everyone (or everything) the conclusion should apply to;
    the <strong>sample</strong> is the part you actually measure; a <strong>census</strong> tries
    to measure the whole population. A census sounds ideal and usually is not: it is expensive,
    slow, and — the fatal flaw — hard to make complete, since the hardest-to-reach people are
    exactly the ones the conclusions most concern. The 1936 US presidential poll is the
    canonical warning: the <em>Literary Digest</em> mailed 10 million ballots, collected 2.4
    million responses, and predicted Landon by a landslide; Roosevelt won by one. The sample was
    huge and wrong, because it was drawn from telephone directories and car registrations during
    the Depression — a coverage bias that selected the affluent — compounded by self-selection
    in who replied. A small, honestly random sample beats a vast, biased one: George Gallup
    called the election within a few points with a few thousand properly chosen respondents. A
    <strong>representative sample</strong> is not "a typical-looking group" hand-picked by a
    researcher — that reintroduces judgment and its biases — but one whose selection mechanism
    gives every member of the population a known chance of inclusion.
  </p>

  <h2>Sampling methods</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Simple random sampling:</strong> every member has an equal chance — the unbiased
    baseline. The classical tool is a <strong>random number table</strong>: number the
    population 001–450, pick a starting point blindly, read three-digit numbers down the table,
    take matches, skip repeats and out-of-range values until n = 30. The same job is now done by
    computer pseudorandom draws, but the table is worth learning because it makes the mechanism
    transparent and auditable. <strong>Systematic sampling:</strong> choose every k-th unit from
    a random start (every 25th name on a register) — operationally easy, nearly equivalent to
    random when the list has no periodic structure, dangerous when it does (sampling every 7th
    day catches only one weekday; sampling houses numbered cyclically along a street misses
    corners). <strong>Stratified sampling:</strong> split the population into strata that matter
    (year groups, regions) and sample randomly within each — it guarantees each stratum's voice
    and shrinks the margin of error, because the between-stratum variance is removed from the
    estimate. <strong>Quota sampling:</strong> fill pre-set quotas (30 women over 60) by
    approaching whoever is convenient — fast and cheap, used when no sampling frame exists
    (street interviews), but the final choice within each quota is human, so the interviewers'
    convenience and approachability biases leak in. The general rule: stratification beats
    quotas beats convenience, and the sampling frame — who could possibly be selected —
    silently defines the population you may generalise to.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a bigger sample fixes a biased one." Bias is a systematic
    error; size only shrinks random error. Ten million biased ballots said Landon. More data
    from the wrong mechanism makes you more precisely wrong — and more confident about it,
    which is worse.
  </p>

  <h2>Asking questions: open versus closed</h2>
  <p>
    Survey questions are measurement instruments, and badly made instruments read zero.
    <strong>Closed questions</strong> (fixed options, rating scales) are quick to answer and
    trivially analysable, but the options themselves frame the response — a yes/no question
    manufactures agreement where a free answer might have been "it depends"; unbalanced scales
    (three positive options, one negative) shift means measurably.
    <strong>Open questions</strong> ("what would improve this service?") capture the unforeseen
    and reveal the vocabulary respondents actually use, at the cost of coding effort and
    coder judgment. The classic failure modes have names: <strong>leading questions</strong>
    ("Do you support the popular measure…?"); <strong>loaded or double-barrelled items</strong>
    ("Do you agree the council should improve safety and reduce waste?" — which half?);
    <strong>order effects</strong> (asking about general trust in journalism before asking
    about a specific outlet changes the answer); <strong>social desirability</strong> (everyone
    reports more reading and less drinking than is true — this is why sensitive topics use
    indirect formats); and <strong>non-attitudes</strong> (about a quarter of respondents will
    offer opinions on entirely fictitious policies rather than say "no view"). Design
    discipline: pilot the instrument, keep items single-barrelled and neutral, randomise option
    order where feasible, and record refusals — "don't know" is data, not noise.
  </p>

  <h2>Classifying data</h2>
  <p>
    The data each variable yields dictates the summary and the test, so classification is
    decided at design time. <strong>Qualitative</strong> (categorical) data sorts into labels:
    nominal categories with no order (blood group, brand) and ordinal ones with order but
    unequal spacing (Agree/Neutral/Disagree, medal classes). <strong>Quantitative</strong>
    data is numerical: <strong>discrete</strong> — countable, gaps between values (number of
    patients, goals scored) — and <strong>continuous</strong> — measured on a continuum, any
    value in an interval (height, reaction time). The classification is a property of the
    measurement, not the storage format (a postcode looks numeric and is nominal). Getting it
    wrong is not pedantry: a mean of ordinal ratings is a category error (medians and modes
    are the honest summaries), bar charts — never histograms — belong to categorical data, and
    the parametric tests of later lessons presume quantitative response scales. Design
    consequence: decide the data type of every variable when you draft the form, because
    "how satisfied are you, 1–5?" and "tell us how satisfied you are" are different instruments
    producing different, non-interconvertible data.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "decimals mean continuous, whole numbers mean discrete."
    Shoe sizes come in halves and are discrete (size 7½ exists; 7.3 does not); reaction times
    get recorded to a thousandth of a second and are continuous. The test is whether
    intermediate values are <em>possible</em> in principle, not whether your instrument
    rounds them away.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "open questions are better — they let people say what they
    really think." They also take more effort to answer, get skipped more often, over-collect
    from the articulate, and yield text that must be coded by judgment — and disagreeing
    coders are measurement error. Professional instruments are mostly closed, with one or two
    open items placed deliberately at the end.
  </p>

  <h2>Fisher's pillars on top of the sampling</h2>
  <p>
    Given a sound sample, the experiment's logic is Fisher's. <strong>Randomisation</strong>
    allocates treatments by chance so that every unmeasured nuisance — soil quality, morning
    versus afternoon, mood, genotype — is distributed between the arms in expectation. It is
    the only mechanism that controls <em>unknown</em> confounders; matching and adjusting
    control only the ones you thought of. <strong>Replication</strong> repeats the comparison
    on enough units to estimate the noise, and the required number is calculable in advance —
    the power calculation. Worked example: you want to detect a 5-point improvement, prior
    studies suggest a standard deviation of 15, and you want 80% power at significance 0.05
    (two-sided). The sample size per group is approximately n = 2(z₀.₉₇₅ + z₀.₈₀)²·σ²/Δ² =
    2 × (1.96 + 0.84)² × 15² / 5² ≈ 2 × 7.84 × 9 ≈ 141 — so about 142 per arm. Run 20 per arm
    and the most likely outcome is a noisy non-result that wastes the entire study.
    <strong>Blocking</strong> handles the nuisance factors you <em>can</em> name: group similar
    units into blocks (same field, same batch, same day) and randomise treatments
    <em>within</em> each block, so block effects cancel in the comparison — Fisher's
    "block what you can, randomise what you cannot". <strong>Factorial designs</strong> vary
    two or more factors simultaneously (fertiliser yes/no × irrigation yes/no): a single 2×2
    trial estimates both main effects and the <em>interaction</em> — fertiliser helps only
    when irrigated — which two separate one-factor experiments are structurally incapable of
    seeing. Add <strong>blinding</strong> (single: subject unaware; double: subject and
    assessor unaware) so expectation effects do not masquerade as treatment effects, and
    <strong>pre-registration</strong> of endpoints and analysis so the plan cannot be
    rewritten after seeing the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "randomising means assigning treatments haphazardly,
    whatever seems fair at the time." Haphazard is human choice in disguise — predictable,
    and therefore biased by the chooser. Randomisation is a protocol: allocation concealed by
    coin, table or computer, performed <em>after</em> a unit is enrolled, so nobody can steer
    who gets what. The distance between "put that one in group B" and "the computer assigned
    that one to B" is the entire discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>The chain in one worked design</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the whole apparatus on a real-scale question: does a daily "retrieval practice"
    app improve exam performance? Population: all GCSE students in the country. Sampling
    frame: schools that volunteer — a frame bias (volunteering schools differ systematically)
    that is acknowledged in the write-up rather than hidden. Stratify the frame by catchment
    type and draw 34 schools; then <strong>cluster</strong>-randomise 17 per arm, because the
    app deploys class by class and individual randomisation would leak across desks.
    Pre-register the primary endpoint: mean score on a standardised, machine-marked test taken
    by every pupil on the same date (blinding of pupils is impossible, so blinding is applied
    where it can be — marking and analysis). Power: the individual-level calculation gave 142
    per arm, but cluster designs pay a design-effect penalty — pupils within a school
    resemble each other — inflating the requirement by 1 + (m − 1)·ICC. With 60 pupils per
    school and an intraclass correlation of 0.1, that is 1 + 59 × 0.1 = 6.9, so 142 × 6.9 ≈
    980 per arm — about 17 schools of 60. Run four schools per arm instead, and the most
    likely outcome is a noisy non-result, however good the app. Every decision above was made
    <em>before</em> the first data point existed — which is the chapter's whole argument
    compressed into one page.
  </p>

  <h2>Blinding, placebos and the observer's bias</h2>
  <p>
    Randomisation balances who gets what; blinding stops the assignment from leaking into the
    measurement. The quantities at stake are not small: placebo analgesia routinely produces
    20–40% of active-drug pain relief in trials, and the <em>expectancy</em> of an energising
    drug produces measurable performance changes even when the pill was inert — so an unblinded
    trial of, say, a stimulant can "find" the drug doing the placebo's work. Single blinding
    (the subject) blocks expectation effects; double blinding (subject and outcome assessor)
    also blocks observer drift — assessors who know the hypothesis rate ambiguous outcomes
    toward it (the classic demonstration: raters measuring the same photographs of children
    shift their developmental scores toward whichever narrative they were told). Even the
    statistical analysis can be blinded: some journals and trials report the analysis run on
    group labels called A and B, unblinded only after the primary result is fixed. Design
    rule: for every point where a human knows the allocation, ask how that knowledge could
    reach the outcome — and close it where you can.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Power is an ethical variable.</strong> An underpowered trial is not merely
    uninformative — among <em>significant</em> results it inflates effect sizes (the winner's
    curse: only lucky overestimates clear the bar) and, across a literature, guarantees a
    false discovery rate far above the nominal 5% once prior odds are low (the
    <em>Lancet</em>-era "most published research findings are false" argument, Ioannidis 2005).
    Design responses now standard in medicine and spreading elsewhere: pre-registration,
    multi-site replication, sequential designs with pre-set stopping rules (which make
    legitimate what "peeking" corrupts in a naive A/B test), and cluster randomisation with the
    design-effect correction (randomise schools rather than pupils, and inflate the required n
    by 1 + (m − 1)·ICC). <strong>Randomisation as the basis of inference:</strong> Fisher's
    deeper point was that the randomisation itself defines the test — the significance of a
    difference can be computed by re-randomising the observed data many times and asking how
    often the gap appears by chance, a <em>randomisation test</em> that needs no normality
    assumption at all and is the conceptual ancestor of modern bootstrap and permutation
    methods. <strong>When experiments are impossible</strong> — you cannot randomise smoking,
    poverty or planet-scale policies — the observational toolkit takes over: matching,
    difference-in-differences, natural experiments (physicians' strikes, lottery wins), and
    the causal-inference machinery of the expert lesson on this ladder. <strong>Open
    questions:</strong> adaptive designs that re-allocate mid-trial, synthetic control methods
    for n = 1 policy changes, and whether preregistration's rigidity costs adaptive
    exploration — the field's current honest tension.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fisher, The Design of Experiments (1935); Box, Hunter and Hunter, Statistics for Experimenters; Squire, "Why the Literary Digest poll failed"; Ioannidis (2005).</p>
